Track 3: Old Man Koster’s Floating Nursery
- Story & Context: Long after the shallow reefs claimed the stranded carrier Paloma, eccentric hermit horticulturist Koster welded pneumatic oboe pipes to the ship’s protruding smokestacks to play alongside every passing vessel at dawn. Navigators crossing the sandbar reported smelling blooming Meyer lemon blossoms long before land appeared, guided solely by the swinging, acoustic double-reed chime echoing off the rusted metal bulkheads. When local coastguards boarded the hulk after his passing, they found his wind-powered horn system still locked into an endless garage shuffle, accompanied by a chalk scrawl on the helm: "Root yourself in iron until the sea air learns to dance." To this day, crews passing the buoy cut their engines to drift in silence, listening for the phantom oboes greeting them through the morning spray.
